WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.856
The Listens table is sixty-four gigabytes.

00:00:04.856 --> 00:00:07.712
Your machine has six point four gigabytes of RAM.

00:00:07.712 --> 00:00:10.568
Ten times too big to load.

00:00:10.568 --> 00:00:13.424
So you break it into RAM-sized chunks and touch one at a time.

00:00:13.424 --> 00:00:16.280
The trick is hash partitioning.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:19.136
Here is how it runs.

00:00:19.136 --> 00:00:23.601
Watch the workspace.

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:28.066
Disk on the left, a small RAM strip in the middle, disk partitions on the right.

00:00:28.066 --> 00:00:32.531
First, reed the input file one page at a time into a RAM input buffer.

00:00:32.531 --> 00:00:36.995
That is all the memory you spend.

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:41.460
Second, hash every row on the key and route it to one of about sixteen output buffers.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.925
Mickey's rows always hash to the same buffer, no matter which page they arrive on.

00:00:45.925 --> 00:00:50.390
Third, when an output buffer fills, flush it to its partition file on disk and clear it.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:54.855
Repeat until the input is consumed.

00:00:54.855 --> 00:00:59.320
One pass.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.785
About sixteen partition files, each one small enough to fit in RAM.

00:01:03.785 --> 00:01:08.249
Sixty-four gigabytes hashed into sixteen partitions of four gigabytes each.

00:01:08.249 --> 00:01:12.714
The same key never splits across two files, so the next stage can sort, join, or group one partition at a time, never the whole table.

00:01:12.714 --> 00:01:17.179
This is not elegance.

00:01:17.179 --> 00:01:21.644
When the table dwarfs RAM, there is no other way to touch all of it.

00:01:21.644 --> 00:01:24.864
And in code, one streaming pass.

00:01:24.864 --> 00:01:28.084
Hash each row to a bucket and spill full buffers to disk.

00:01:28.084 --> 00:01:31.304
Equal keys always land in the same bucket.

00:01:31.304 --> 00:01:35.985
The cost is one reed of every page and one write of every page.

00:01:35.985 --> 00:01:40.666
Two N total.

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:45.348
Linear in the data size.

00:01:45.348 --> 00:01:50.029
A RAM-blind join of two big tables is quadratic, order N squared.

00:01:50.029 --> 00:01:54.710
One partitioning pass makes every downstream sort, join, and group by linear instead, because each partition now fits in RAM and is touched once.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:58.741
Divide and conquer.

00:01:58.741 --> 00:02:02.772
Split a big problem into small ones, then process terabytes with gigabytes of RAM at linear cost.

00:02:02.772 --> 00:02:06.803
As we will see later, these underpin most modern data systems, including Postgres, BigQuery, and Spark.

00:02:06.803 --> 00:02:10.834
Next, the same trick applied to sorting.

00:02:10.834 --> 00:02:14.865
BigSort.
